":false,"pf":12.0,"pa":20.0,"off_snaps":62,"epa_per_play":-0.2167,"pass_rate":0.6935,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6471,"def_epa_per_play":-0.1956},{"week":16,"opponent":"SF","home":true,"pf":29.0,"pa":17.0,"off_snaps":63,"epa_per_play":0.1649,"pass_rate":0.5397,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5789,"def_epa_per_play":0.06002},{"week":17,"opponent":"CLE","home":false,"pf":20.0,"pa":3.0,"off_snaps":55,"epa_per_play":-0.098,"pass_rate":0.5273,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6061,"def_epa_per_play":-0.365},{"week":18,"opponent":"NYJ","home":false,"pf":20.0,"pa":32.0,"off_snaps":68,"epa_per_play":-0.1281,"pass_rate":0.6618,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5926,"def_epa_per_play":0.06014}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2022,"team":"SF","distance":0.8538,"shared":21,"play_caller":"Kyle Shanahan"},{"season":2025,"team":"ATL","distance":1.117,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Zac Robinson"},{"season":2023,"team":"BAL","distance":1.165,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Todd Monken"},{"season":2023,"team":"CHI","distance":1.176,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2023,"team":"SF","distance":1.216,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Kyle Shanahan"}],"defense":[{"season":2023,"team":"SF","distance":0.6805,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"CIN","distance":0.7282,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Lou Anarumo"},{"season":2025,"team":"NE","distance":0.7674,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"CHI","distance":0.7737,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2018,"team":"BUF","distance":0.7815,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Dolphins went 8-9 in 2024 and were outscored 364 to 345. The defense did most of the work, finishing 9th in EPA allowed while the offense ranked 21st in EPA per play. What set them apart was the 2nd highest 21 personnel usage and the 2nd highest Cover 4 rate in the league.","offense":"The Dolphins offense was a little below average by EPA per play, and it was much better passing the ball than running it. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the 2nd highest 21 personnel usage and the 2nd highest pistol usage in the league. Tua Tagovailoa ran the passing game, with Tyreek Hill leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Dolphins defense was well above average by EPA allowed per play. Its strongest area was pressure rate, where it ranked 7th, and its weakest was sack rate, where it ranked 26th. Zach Sieler was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Chop Robinson, who had 6 sacks while playing only 53% of the snaps, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Schematically, they had the 2nd highest Cover 4 rate in the league.","special":"The Dolphins were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 90% of field goals, which was well above average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into receivers, about 21% of the money on the books. Their secondary was among the oldest in the league.","players":"Tua Tagovailoa played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 2,867 yards. Targets were spread around, with Tyreek Hill and Jonnu Smith leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. The backfield was a committee led by Devon Achane and Raheem Mostert. On defense, Zach Sieler led the team with 10 sacks and Tyrel Dodson picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"Mike McDaniel called the offense, and the defensive play caller for this season is not in the coaching data. Their offense looked most like the 2022 49ers under Kyle Shanahan and the 2025 Falcons under Zac Robinson. Their defense looked most like the 2023 49ers and the 2024 Bengals under Lou Anarumo.","trends":"The offense went from 4th in EPA per play in 2023 to 21st in 2024, a big step back. The defense moved from 15th in EPA allowed per play in 2023 to 9th in 2024, a modest improvement. The biggest change in approach was Cover 2 rate, which went from 7% to 28%.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2024. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}